        <h1 class="sr-only">Safety</h1>

        <div id="safety-overview" class="content-card">
          <figure class="page-mascot page-mascot--safety" aria-hidden="true">
            <img src="../static/image/any-icon/extension/safety.png" alt="" width="440" height="464"
              loading="lazy" decoding="async">
          </figure>
          <h2>Overview</h2>
          <p>The laboratory is the core setting for scientific discovery and engineering innovation. Many advances that have transformed human health, the environment and society were born in laboratories, yet the laboratory is also a space that demands unusually strict standards of discipline and responsibility. We therefore hold that safety is not an add-on requirement outside the experiment: it is part of experimental design, execution and record-keeping. Every member who enters the laboratory must be responsible to themselves, to their teammates, to the laboratory and to the environment, and must treat every activity in the laboratory seriously.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-risk-matrix" class="content-card">
          <h2>LS Risk Matrix</h2>
          <p>This table uses an LS (Likelihood × Severity) risk matrix to carry out an item-by-item safety assessment of the project's experimental workflow <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-1">[1]</a><a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-2">[2]</a>. L denotes likelihood: the probability that the risk event occurs under the existing training, facilities, SOPs and protective conditions. S denotes severity: the consequence for human health, experimental progress and the environment once it occurs. L and S are each graded from 1 to 5, and the risk value is L × S: 1–4 low risk, 5–9 general risk, 10–16 moderately high risk, 17–25 high risk. The table also lists the initial risk, the existing control measures and the residual risk; the L and S of every item carry an independent scoring rationale rather than a blanket score, so each score corresponds one-to-one with a specific operation, material and set of control conditions.</p>
          <p>When reading the table, the risk value is the initial risk before control measures are applied, and the residual risk is what remains after training, SOPs, PPE, clean benches, fume hoods, SDS consultation and waste inactivation have been applied. We assessed 15 experimental steps, covering HA hemagglutinin handling, BY4741 yeast culture and passaging, transformation, CRISPR-Cas9 knockout of FAR1/SST2, Gpa1-Gaq homologous recombination, PAGER receptor expression, DCZ1/DCZ small-molecule agonist handling, X-α-Gal and MEL1 colorimetric detection, yEGFP fluorescence detection, centrifugation and glassware handling, autoclave and UV equipment, the air-sampling interface, waste collection and inactivation, routine laboratory management, and the environmental release risk of the engineered yeast. Most steps carry an initial risk value of 1–2 and fall in the low-risk band. The highest risk value is DCZ1/DCZ small-molecule agonist handling, with L=2 and S=2 giving a risk value of 4, still within the low-risk band; the reason is that handling small-molecule chemicals may occasionally involve skin contact or inhalation, but after consulting the SDS, wearing gloves and goggles, working in a fume hood or clean bench and segregating waste liquids, the residual risk drops to low.</p>

