        <div id="safety-lab-environment" class="content-card">
          <h2>Laboratory Safety Environment</h2>
          <p>The laboratory we work in has strict and well-developed safety management. Entry is controlled by access authorization, the areas are clearly divided, and operations of different risk levels are carried out in their corresponding areas. Warning signs for biological hazards, chemical hazards, flammability, corrosion, autoclaving, ultraviolet light, eyewash, first aid, fire extinguishers, emergency exits and waste segregation are posted throughout the laboratory, and SOPs, emergency contacts and safety reminders are posted at key locations <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-2">[2]</a>. A Safety Emergency Response Plan for Teaching Laboratories (Trial) is also posted in a prominent position, compiled in accordance with the Work Safety Law, the Fire Protection Law and the Emergency Response Law of the People's Republic of China and the Regulations on the Safety Management of Hazardous Chemicals; it sets out the emergency organizational structure, the escalation and reporting procedure and 24-hour emergency contact numbers (Figure 1). After entering the laboratory we can quickly identify the sources of risk, the boundaries of operation and the location of emergency equipment.</p>
          <p>The laboratory is equipped with clean benches, fume hoods, an autoclave, an eyewash station, an emergency shower, first-aid kits, fire extinguishers, spill kits, absorbent materials, sharps containers, biohazard waste bags and chemical waste containers. Flammable liquids and other hazardous chemicals are kept in a dedicated storage cabinet with the corresponding SDS documentation on hand, and hazardous waste is placed in segregated collection containers and never poured away at will (Figure 3). The eyewash station and emergency shower (Figure 2) are always available. For microbiological work we operate inside a clean bench; for volatile or irritant chemicals we work in a fume hood (Figure 5). Our supervising teachers give safety reminders tailored to each specific experiment, and team members also remind and check one another. After experiments we handle biological waste and chemical waste by category and autoclave materials that need inactivation. Overall, the laboratory's level of safety, its warning system and its emergency provision give us a reliable basis for carrying out this project.</p>
          {% for fig in safety.figures %}
          <figure class="safety-figure">
            <img src="{{ basePath }}{{ fig.src }}" alt="{{ fig.alt }}" width="{{ fig.width }}" height="{{ fig.height }}" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
            <figcaption>{{ fig.caption }}{% if fig.note %}<span class="safety-figure-note">{{ fig.note }}</span>{% endif %}</figcaption>
          </figure>
          {% endfor %}
        </div>

        <div id="safety-personal" class="content-card">
          <h2>Personal Safety</h2>
          <p>All our members have received laboratory safety training and passed the laboratory access requirements <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-1">[1]</a><a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-2">[2]</a>. We are familiar with the laboratory safety rules, emergency procedures and personal protective requirements. Before any laboratory activity in this project began, our supervising teachers gave us an additional targeted safety briefing, focusing on the biological materials involved, the risks of the reagents, the boundaries of operation and the record-keeping requirements. During the experiments our activities were recorded and reported, and we had studied the reagents we used in advance to confirm their risks.</p>
          <p>For personal protection we select and correctly wear lab coats, gloves, goggles and masks according to the risk of the specific operation, keep proper working practice inside a clean bench or fume hood, wash our hands properly before and after experiments, and take off our lab coats before leaving the laboratory. We all know where the eyewash station, first-aid kit, fire extinguisher, spill kit and emergency shower are, and in the event of a spill, contact or suspected exposure we know how to respond immediately and report it. During experiments we supervise one another's protective equipment and working state, keep records complete, and never carry out high-risk operations alone. Overall, personal safety is not a paper rule for us but a practical habit and a team consensus in daily experiments.</p>
        </div>

        <div id="safety-project" class="content-card">
          <h2>Project Safety</h2>
          <p>The target detected in this project is the hemagglutinin (HA) protein on the surface of influenza A virus. The HA hemagglutinin used in our experiments is purchased; it is not infectious and does not possess the physiological activity of influenza A virus. An influenza vaccine based on recombinant HA protein contains only hemagglutinin as a single antigenic protein and no viral genome, so it cannot self-replicate and is itself non-infectious <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-3">[3]</a>. The HA hemagglutinin used in this experiment therefore does not produce the infectious characteristics of influenza A virus, cannot infect people and cannot make people ill. We do not handle live virus, we introduce no replication-competent viral sequences, and we introduce no virulence-related genes.</p>
          <p>For the recognition element we use an anti-HA nanobody (VHH) as the recognition domain <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-4">[4]</a>, and the human hM1Dq receptor as the signalling core <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-5">[5]</a>. The PAGER (Programmable Antigen-gated G-protein-coupled Engineered Receptors) receptor uses an "antigen-gated de-repression" logic <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-6">[6]</a>: in the absence of HA, the MT1 auto-inhibitory peptide occupies the orthosteric site of hM1Dq and holds the receptor at a low background; once HA binds the nanobody, steric hindrance relieves the MT1 inhibition, and the DCZ small molecule then activates hM1Dq <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-7">[7]</a>, triggering the downstream yeast MAPK pathway and FUS1 reporter gene output. This self-inhibitory design reduces background leakage and false-positive risk <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-6">[6]</a>.</p>
          <p>For the chassis we use the non-pathogenic, laboratory-standard <em>Saccharomyces cerevisiae</em> BY4741. <em>S. cerevisiae</em> is classed at the lowest risk level in national biosafety listings, and this project is carried out entirely under biosafety level 1 (BSL-1) conditions <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-1">[1]</a>. BY4741 is an internationally used laboratory chassis with a well-defined genetic background; it is naturally auxotrophic for histidine (His), leucine (Leu), methionine (Met) and uracil (Ura), cannot synthesise these four essential nutrients and must obtain them from outside <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-8">[8]</a>. The vector system, the reporter system and the plasmids used for GPA1 humanisation/knockout constructed in this study carry LEU, HIS and URA selection markers respectively, complementing the corresponding auxotrophies.</p>
          <p>To ensure biosafety we also designed a marker-removal strategy: the URA marker is removed by 5-fluoroorotic acid (5-FOA) counter-selection <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-9">[9]</a>, returning the engineered strain to a uracil-auxotrophic state after modification, which together with the chassis's original histidine, leucine and methionine auxotrophies makes it a quadruple auxotroph. This multiple-auxotrophy mechanism makes it difficult for the strain to proliferate effectively in environments lacking the corresponding nutrient supplements; conditions that supply all four free nutrients simultaneously and at sufficient concentration do not normally exist in the natural environment, so our engineered yeast survives and proliferates mainly in specially formulated media.</p>
          <p>This design — genetically modifying an already auxotrophic strain and layering a marker-removal step on top — makes it difficult for the strain to proliferate effectively outside the specific supplemented medium, thereby reducing the risk of environmental release. It should be noted that auxotrophy is usually regarded as the first layer of biological containment, and its protective strength depends on the availability of the essential nutrients in the surrounding environment <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-10">[10]</a>; work elsewhere has pushed yeast escape frequencies below 10⁻⁹ by introducing stronger barriers such as dependence on an unnatural amino acid. We therefore do not rest the whole safety case on auxotrophy itself, but also adopt enclosed operation, autoclave inactivation of waste and standardised management, forming multiple layers of protection.</p>
          <p>For chassis modification we use the CRISPR-Cas9 system <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-11">[11]</a> to knock out FAR1 <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-12">[12]</a> and SST2 <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-13">[13]</a>, removing interference from the yeast's own mating pathway with the cell cycle and signal strength; we also designed a Gpa1-Gaq chimeric G protein, replacing the five C-terminal amino acids KIGII of yeast Gpa1 with EYNLV from human Gαq <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-14">[14]</a>, so that hM1Dq can couple to the yeast MAPK pathway. The reporter module uses the pheromone-responsive FUS1 promoter <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-15">[15]</a> to drive yEGFP <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-16">[16]</a> or MEL1 <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-17">[17]</a> expression, producing green fluorescence or a blue colour respectively. These modifications target only signal transduction and reporter output; they introduce no pathogenic factors and do not enhance the yeast's pathogenicity or environmental fitness.</p>
          <p>All cultures, consumables and waste containing the engineered yeast are collected according to the laboratory's biological waste rules and autoclaved or otherwise effectively inactivated before disposal. Waste liquids containing chemical reagents are collected separately and handed to a qualified institution. The whole system is modular: replacing only the nanobody domain would allow it to be extended to the detection of other respiratory pathogens <a class="safety-cite" href="#safety-reference-6">[6]</a>, and the safety assessment will be updated in step with any change of target.</p>
        </div>

